  ],
  "text": "Inside a damaged reactor, at the bottom of a trench, or on another planet, a robot is the only worker available. Not because it is better. Because nothing else can be there at all. Deep sea vehicles work at pressures that would crush a person instantly. Decommissioning machines work in radiation that would kill anyone in minutes. But the hardest constraint is not pressure or radiation. It is delay. A signal to Mars takes somewhere between four and twenty four minutes, each way. So you cannot steer a rover. By the time you see the rock, you have already hit it. Which makes autonomy out there not a luxury feature. It is the only option available. You send a destination and it works out the route by itself, with nobody to ask. Everywhere else, autonomy is a convenience. Out there it is the entire job. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
